\". We can keep that.\n\nNow we need to decide whether to include \"corrected_claim\". The original envelope includes a corrected_claim with a candidate tuple [0,0,0,0] (example). We can replace it with a candidate tuple that is the same as the original claim? Or we could keep the original claim as candidate? The instructions say \"An optional corrected_claim is an uncommitted candidate, not a replacement.\" So we could include a corrected_claim that is a candidate tuple (maybe the same). But we could also omit it? The envelope template includes it; we should include it. We can set corrected_claim to the same claim as original (i.e., the tuple [1,0,3,1]) but with status \"candidate\". That would be a candidate version of the claim. That seems appropriate.\n\nThus we need to fill corrected_claim with claim_id \"c1-tuples\", dependencies referencing problem:csp-6412, provenance with message_id \"csp-6412-c4-reviewer-1\", revision 1, state_revision 1, status \"candidate\", value with component \"c1\", tuples containing the tuple [1,0,3,1], variables list.\n\nNow we need to ensure that the \"target\" field references the claim being responded to: resource_id \"claim:c1-tuples\", revision 1, state_revision 1.\n\nNow \"read_refs\" includes the problem and the claim (revision 1). That's fine.\n\nNow \"recipients\": [\"shared-integrator\"] (as in template). \"reply_to\": \"csp-6412-c1-proposer-1\". \"schema_version\": 1. \"sender\": \"c4-reviewer\". \"problem_id\": \"csp-6412\".\n\nThus we need to output this JSON.\n\nNow we need to verify that the claim we propose (the corrected_claim) is indeed a candidate that is consistent with constraints. It is the same tuple as original claim, which we have shown is consistent. So we can propose that as a corrected claim.\n\nThus the envelope will have intent \"REPORT_EVIDENCE\"? Or \"CHALLENGE\"? The instruction says to use CHALLENGE only if you found a specific issue; otherwise REPORT_EVIDENCE. So we should set intent to \"REPORT_E